```js caption="An escaping helper that knows two characters, used inside an attribute"
const esc = (s) => s.replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");

const name = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("name") || "amigo";
const button = document.querySelector("#cta");

button.innerHTML = `<span title="${esc(name)}">Comprar</span>`;
// ?name=x%22%20onmouseover%3D%22alert(1)  →  <span title="x" onmouseover="alert(1)">
```

`esc` never touches the quote, and in a quoted attribute the delimiter is the quote, not the angle bracket. The value `x" onmouseover="alert(1)` closes `title` early, `onmouseover="alert(1)"` parses as a second attribute of the `<span>`, and the handler runs as soon as the pointer enters the button. Escaping `<` and `>` is not wrong, it is just irrelevant here: neither character is the one that breaks out.

Three ways out, in increasing robustness:

- **Encode for the attribute context.** The helper has to cover what can end the attribute and what the parser decodes: `&`, `<`, `>`, `"` and `'`. That same helper is still wrong in a URL sink, which is why the fix is per context and not a fixed set of characters.
- **Do not build HTML from a string.** Create the node and assign the value: `const span = document.createElement("span"); span.title = name; span.textContent = "Comprar";`. A property or `setAttribute` does not reparse markup, so a quote is just a character.
- **Sanitize the final string** with a maintained parser-based library, if the product really needs HTML authored by users.

The bug is not the escaping call, it is the assumption behind it: a single helper for every context. `innerHTML` reparses the whole string, so every byte the helper left alone is markup again.

